## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Undoubtedly, the usage of fossil fuels, including coal, oil, and natural gas, has motivated the development of human civilization in the past decades. However, the overdependence on fossil fuels will lead to disastrous consequences that humankind must to deal with.[1-2] For one thing, the massive consumption of non-renewable and regional fossil fuels will bring inevitable energy shortage as the development of human society in the coming decades.[3] For another, the large quantities of emissions of greenhouse gas, such as $\mathsf{C O}_{2},$ arising from the usage of fossil fuels are the predominant reasons for severe environmental pollution and global warming.[4-6] Therefore, many countries, recognizing the drawbacks of greenhouse gas, are being committed to reach global peaking of greenhouse gas emissions as soon as possible.[7] For example, the Chinese government has announced that it will peak carbon emissions by 2030 and become carbon neutral by 2060. The replacement of fossil fuels with renewable and clean energy, like wind energy, tidal energy, electric energy, nuclear energy and solar energy, is an effective way to complete the task.[8] Among these renewable and clean energies, solar energy is the most abundant and reliable for human beings on the planet. Therefore, the synthetic systems with efficient solar energy conversion have attracted wide attentions in recent years.
To build the efficient artificial solar energy conversion systems, one way is to study and mimic the natural photosynthesis systems such as those in plants and cyanobacteria, which have been working for millions of years to convert ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and solar energy to specific biomass for metabolism.[9] These systems typically consist of ordered organization and spatially separated light-harvesting chromophores and subtle biocatalysts to direct complex enzymatic reactions. Typically, the natural photosynthesis, a process of solar-driven reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to produce carbohydrates and the oxidation of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ to produce $\mathsf{O}_{2},$ occurs through the cooperation of photosystem I (PS I) and photosystem II (PS II).[3,10] As shown in Figure 1, PSI and PS II absorb light energy, ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ can be oxidized in PS II to produce $\mathsf{O}_{2},$ then the electrons left in PS II can transfer via cytochrome b6f to PS I, the electron transport chain is also known as the Hill and Bendall Z-scheme, ends with PS I delivering electrons to generate NADH from $\mathsf{N A D}^{+}$ for sustained ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction.[11-13]
Inspired by the natural photosynthesis, the artificial photosynthesis (AP) has motivated the development of photocatalytic devices based on organic and inorganic solid-state materials.[14-16] Though solid-state materials tend to have efficient light harvesting ability, the specificity, selectivity and self-repair properties of natural photosynthesis for complex products are higher than those of artificial photosynthesis, integrating enzymes and synthetic materials to construct semi-artificial photosynthesis (SAP) systems, where the two components can work in synergy to promote the solar energy conversion due to their higher catalytic activity and stability, has recently attracted new research interest.[17-19] Similar to the case of natural photosynthesis, AP and SAP follow the same fundamental processes to convert solar energy into chemical fuels: i) light harvesting, (ii) efficient spatial separation and fast migration of charges to catalytic centers, and (iii) redox catalysis.[8,14,20] In general, semiconductor materials are preferred as photocatalysts because of their light harvesting and charges separation properties, such as $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ and so on.[21-22] However, fully mimicking the natural photosynthesis process is challenging for both AP and SAP, because both ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ oxidation and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction involve multiple physical and chemical reaction processes with high reaction barriers, which requires not only efficient photosensitizers and considerable charges separation, but also highly active catalysts, especially for artificial photosynthesis system with a single phase photocatalyst.[23] Even though the catalytic efficiency of homogeneous artificial photosynthesis system has been comparable with that of natural photosynthesis system,[24-26] homogeneous systems suffer from the inevitable defect of poor long-term stability.[27] It is still a challenge for heterogeneous artificial photosynthesis system to achieve desired catalytic efficiency, therefore, many strategies have been proposed to integrate photosensitizer and active catalyst components into a single solid material to construct heterogeneous photosynthesis system, for example, supramolecular assemblies ,[28] but limited by the involved multi-step syntheses.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of porous crystalline materials consisting of metallic nodes (metal ions or clusters) and organic linkers, have been widely considered to be one of the most promising platforms for artificial photosynthesis and semiartificial photosynthesis due to their large specific surface area, high porosity, and the capability of precisely designing and tailoring structures at an atomic level for integrating photosensitizer and catalytic sites into a single catalyst.[29-35] Generally, when MOFs are used as photocatalysts in artificial photosynthesis systems, the chromophores among MOFs usually act as photosensitizers to absorb photons, then electrons are excited from the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of the ligands leaving holes in the HOMO and resulting in separated photogenerated charges, namely the ligand-localized exciton can be converted into a charge-separate state.[36] The photogenerated charges can be transferred to the metal nodes by the classical ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) mechanism, thereby participating in the redox reactions.[37-38] Nevertheless, it is difficult to achieve fascinating catalytic activity in artificial photosynthesis systems for opportunistic MOFs due to the poor light absorption, [39-40] lack of accessible catalytic active sites,[41] the low transfer efficiency of carriers results in the rapid recombination of photogenerated electrons and holes,[42-43] and the inappropriate electronic structure limits the occurrence of carbon dioxide reduction and water oxidation.[44]
Here, we provide a comprehensive review of the research progress in MOF-based artificial and semi-artificial photosynthesis. Given the artificial photosynthesis processes and the advantages and disadvantages of MOF-based photocatalysts, the common design strategies of MOF-based photocatalysts were summarized in order to improve their activity and selectivity in artificial photosynthesis. The examples showing the applications of MOF-based photocatalytic systems, especially photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction and ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution, were reviewed. A perspective of the challenges and future research directions of MOF-based artificial and semi-artificial photosynthesis systems were discussed.
## 2. Design Strategies of AP Systems Based on MOFs
Artificial photosynthesis involved multiple charge transfer processes, an efficient photocatalyst should be superior in many kinetic aspects, such as adsorption and activation of reactants, photogenerated charge separation and transfer, and the subsequent charge utilization, in addition to thermodynamically permitting the reactions that occur. MOFs featuring large specific surface area, high porosity, and the capability of precisely designing and tailoring structures at an atomic level endow their unique opportunity to integrate photosensitizers, reactants adsorption sites and catalytic sites into a single catalyst, simultaneously optimizing both the thermodynamics and kinetics for efficient artificial photosynthesis. So far, many strategies have been developed to obtain efficient MOF-based photocatalysts by means of optimizing the factors affecting photosynthetic performance, such as light absorption, photogenerated charge separation and transfer, catalytic active sites, and so on.
## 2.1. Enhancement of light absorption
Lead halide perovskite (LHP), featuring large extinction coefficient, wide light absorption range and long photogenerated carrier lifetime, has been designed as photosensitizer, usually encapsulated in the pores of MOFs, to trigger the light harvesting, and the metal centers can serve as an electron reservoir to accept photogenerated electrons transferring from LHP and subsequently taicotn.a[s67-a6n8 $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ $(\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3})$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ eraoctvisvkaitieonquaanndtruemdudctoitosn(rQeDacs)- were encapsulated in PCN-221 $\left({\mathsf{F e}}_{x}\right)$ $(x=0{-}1)$ by a sequential deposition route (Figure 3) to construct $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}@$ PCN-221 $\left\langle\mathsf{F e}_{x}\right\rangle$ $\scriptstyle(x=0-1)$ , exhibiting a record-high total yield of $1559\ \mathsf{m m o l{\cdot}g}^{-1}$ for photocatalytic CO2 reduction to CO (34%) and CH4 (66%).[67] ${\mathsf{C s P b}}{\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{r}}_{3}$ quantum dots were encapsulated in the pores of ZIF-67 and ZIF-8 by in situ synthetic procedure. The $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}@21\mathsf{F}{-}8$ and $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}@2!\mathsf{F}{-}67$ composite catalysts show enhanced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction activity with electron consumption rates of 15.498 and 29.630 μmol·g−1·h−1, respectively.[68]
## 2.2. Accelerate the charge separation and transfer
The MOFs based artificial photosynthesis requires an efficient photogenerated charges transfer process to minimize their immediate recombination, which means adequate electrons or holes are available for further redox reactions, always leading to impressive photosynthetic activity.[69-70] In general, rapid charges separation and transfer can be realized, which spatially separate charges to prevent recombination, by optimizing or shortening the transfer pathway during the process that photogenerated electrons or holes transfer from photosensitive unit to catalytic active sites.[10,71] Until now, many MOFs have been found to have good opportunities for photogenerated charge separation. For example, the moderate structural defects or electron trap states in MOFs [72-73] and MOFs with mixed-valency metal nodes in frameworks that exhibit a through-bond intervalence charge transfer (lVCT) mechanism can facilitate the charge separation.[74] Moreover, the incorporation of MOFs with other materials, such as metal nanoparticles[75-79] and semiconductor materials,[75,80-84] to construct heterogeneous composite photocatalysts, in which photogenerated charges can transfer from one material to another, can also lead to the efficient charge separation.
Considering that most of the photoexcited electrons would be annihilated during transport to the photocatalytic active centers for reaction, it can be realized that the faster electrons are captured by the acceptor/cocatalyst, the higher photocatalytic activity. The spatial position of electron acceptor has significant influence on MOF-based photocatalysts. As proposed by Jiang et al., Pt nanoparticles of ca. 3 nm are deliberately encapsulated inside or supported on MOF to afford $P\mathrm{t}@\mathsf{U i O}_{-}66.\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{P t/U i O{-}}66\mathsf{N H}_{2},$ respectively, in which the only difference is the spatial position of Pt, as illustrated in Figure 4.[85] For Pt-decorated UiO- $66\ –\mathsf{N H}_{2},$ the photogenerated electrons on the MOF can transfer to Pt through the Schottky junction for the subsequent proton reduction, because the Pt with low overpotential can act as electron trap and provide active sites for hydrogen evolution. The path of electron transfer from MOF to embedded Pt is greatly shorter than that to loaded Pt, which favors the electrons transportation and retards the undesired recombination of electrons and holes, thereby yielding much higher photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity than $\mathsf{P t/U i O{-}}66{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ . The underlying electron-transfer mechanism was unveiled by ultrafast transient absorption and photoluminescence spectroscopy. In addition, the $P\mathsf{t}@\mathsf{U i O}\mathsf{-}66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ sample possesses more excellent stability and recyclability as a result of the great confinement for Pt NPs in the MOF.
Recently, piezo-photocatalysis over MOFs has become a research focus. Under the action of mechanical tension or strain in the asymmetric direction, the piezoelectric MOF deforms and the center of positive/negative charges in the unit-cell displace, leading to spontaneous polarization. As a result, positive and negative charges are generated on opposite surfaces, resulting in a built-in electric field, which is beneficial to charge separation. For example, $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}(2\mathsf{r})$ and UiO- ${\cdot}66N{\mathsf{H}}_{2}({\mathsf{H f}})$ were adopted for piezo-photocatalysis by Jiang et al., for which both MOFs, featuring the same structures except for diverse $Z r/H f$ -oxo clusters, possess distinctly different piezoelectric properties.[86] To determine the piezoelectric properties, the surface potential images of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathsf{H f})$ were measured in the dark and under visible light irradiation by Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM). An inward built-in electric field can be formed inside UiO-66- ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}({\mathsf{H f}})$ under the stress of the probe tip, resulting in a positive surface voltage of up to $54.4~\mathrm{mV},$ which proved its piezoelectric property (Figure 5a, b). However, U $\mathsf{I i O-66-N H}_{2}(2\mathsf{r})$ exhibited weaker piezoelectric response, investigated by the same methods. Under the sweep bias from $^{-5}$ to ${\mathsf{5}}{\mathsf{V}},$ the phase changes are ${\approx}100^{\circ}$ and ${\approx}180^{\circ}$ in the piezo response phase hysteresis loops of UiO-6 $S\mathrm{-NH}_{2}(Z\Gamma)$ and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}(\mathsf{H f}),$ respectively, and typical butterfly shapes can be observed in the amplitude loops of the MOFs, but their maximum amplitudes vary greatly, which were further investigated by piezo response force microscopy (PFM). The piezo response phase hysteresis loops and amplitude butterfly loops of UiO-66-NH2(Hf) were shown in Figures 5c, d. Then, the maximum effective piezoelectric coefficient $d_{33}$ was calculated according to the slope of the amplitude loops, the $d_{33}$ values of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}(2\mathsf{r})$ and UiO-66- ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}({\mathsf{H f}})$ are ${\sim}4$ and $139~\mathsf{p m}\cdot\mathsf{V}^{-1}.$ , respectively. The results showed that UiO- $66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathsf{H f})$ possesses a much higher piezoelectric response than UiO-66- $N H_{2}(Z r)$ . The photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}}(\mathsf{Z r})$ and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}(H f)}$ with Pt nanoparticles (NPs) as the cocatalyst introduced by photo-deposition was examined. The sample $\mathsf{P t/U i O{-}\ell66{-}N H_{2}(Z r)}$ and $\mathsf{P t/U i O{-}}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}(H f)}$ exhibited negligible activity differences in photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production, by contrast, the latter exhibited 2.2 times higher activity while the former almost remains upon further ultrasonic irradiation, which should be attributed to the stronger piezoelectric effect of $\mathsf{U i O G6-N H}_{2}(\mathsf{H f})$ . This is because upon simultaneous light and ultrasonic irradiation, the photogenerated electrons and holes are driven by the internal piezoelectric potential to the opposite directions/sides, thereby improving the photocatalytic activity of $\mathsf{P t/U i O{-}}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}(H f)}$ .
## 2.3. Introduce additional active units
The MOF-based photocatalysts with a single catalytic active center often show mediocre catalytic efficiency and selectivity, and the relevant reaction mechanism is elusive.[62] Despite the (2D) MOFs[87-88] or by controlling the crystal plane in three-di- fact that more active sites can be obtained in two-dimensional mensional (3D) MOFs,[89] the resulting enhancement of photocatalytic activity is usually limited. The introduction of additional active units, such as metals, metal oxides, metal cluster, enzymes and so on, by means of encapsulating, loading or grafting into the pore channel, surface, or linker/node of MOFs provides great possibilities for efficient and highly selective photocatalysis of MOF-based catalysts due to the intriguing synergistic properties between MOF and the additional active center.[90-96]
To meet the demand of efficient photocatalysis, the photosensitive units and catalytic sites in proximity, facilitating a rapider electron transfer, would be preferred. Further, it is of great significance and benefit to downsize the common metal nanoparticles $({\mathsf{N P s}})$ cocatalysts to highly supported single-atom catalysts (SACs) with good accessibility. Jiang et al. adopted $Z r$ -based MOFs, because the $2r_{6}{-0\times0}$ cluster with neighboring surface $-0/0\mathsf{H}_{\mathbf{x}}$ sites can provide lone-pair electrons and charge balance to immobilize the extraneous metal species, for the construction of SACs.[71] Employed by a rapid and facile microwave-assisted method, diverse metal ions $(\mathsf{N i}^{2+},\mathsf{C o}^{2+},\mathsf{C u}^{2+},\mathsf{R u}^{3+})$ were bound to $Z r_{6^{-}}\circ\times0$ clusters in different MOFs, followed by hydroxylation, sulfidation, or further oxidation, affording MOF supported SACs with a tunable coordination environment. Among them, as a representative, ${\mathsf{N i}}_{1}{-}{\mathsf{X}}/{\mathsf{M O F}}$ $M O F=\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-}N H_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{1}-\mathsf{X}=\mathsf{N i}_{1}-\mathsf{S},$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{-O}$ $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{-S}_{\mathsf{o x}};$ $\mathsf{o x}=$ oxidation) (Figure 6) with a series of high-loading $(>4\ \mathsf{w t}\%)$ SACs featured a modulated microenvironment around the $\mathsf{N i}_{1}$ sites on $Z r_{6}–0\times0$ clusters. The obtained single-atom $\mathsf{N i}_{1}$ catalyst, showing the highest atomic availability and close proximity to the linkers (photosensitive unit) that is benefitial to charge transfer, is far superior to that of corresponding NiS or Ni NPs supported by the MOF. Moreover, among the ${\mathsf{N i}}_{1}{-}{\mathsf{X}}/{\mathsf{M O F}}$ photocatalysts with different coordination microenvironments of $\mathsf{N i}_{1},$ the ${\mathsf{N i}}_{1}{-}{\mathsf{S}}/{\mathsf{M O F}}$ with a unique Ni(I) microenvironment involving sulfur-coordinated Ni(I) sites possesses the highest charge separation efficiency and the lowest proton activation barrier, thereby presenting excellent photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production activity, up to 270-times of the pristine MOF and far surpassing the other $\mathsf{N i}_{1}$ −X/ MOF counterparts.
Zhang et al. adopted Eu-MOF, $(\mathsf{D M A})_{2}[\mathsf{E u}_{6}(\upmu_{3}\mathsf{-O H})_{8}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}.$ $(\mathsf{b p v d c})_{6}]\cdot8\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C H}_{2}\mathsf{O H}\cdot7\mathsf{D M F}$ (Eu-bpy, bpydc $=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylate), as a molecular platform to integrate ${\mathsf{R u}}({\mathsf{b}}{\mathsf{p}}\forall)_{3}$ photosensitizer (Eu-bpy-Ru, bpy $=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) and monometallic catalysts [bpy-CuCl/X] $({\mathsf X}={\mathsf C}{\mathsf I}$ or adenine) for solar-driven ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion (Figure 7a).[97] The coordination environments of Eu centers and monometallic copper catalytic active sites can be precisely modulated for the composite. The coordination environment and the valence of Cu center in the MOFs were determined by EXAFS and XANES. The results showed that the Cu(II) center in Eu-bpy- $\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}$ was in a near-square-planar geometry completed by two chlorides and two N atoms from one bpy ligand with average $c\mathsf{u}{-}\mathsf{C l}$ and $c u-N$ bond lengths of 2.24 and $\overset{\cdot}{1.99\mathrm{\AA}}.$ respectively. Further, Eu-bpy-CuCl/adenine was obtained by introducing adenine molecule into the MOF via replacing one ${\mathsf{C l}}^{-}$ ion due to that the adenime-based MOFs can not only show high and selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake but also possess potential N sites to form a H-bond with the Cu bound formate intermediate. After the mixed ligand synthetic and post-synthetic process, the efficient multielectron injection from antenna molecules to copper catalytic active sites to drive highly selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction can be achieved in the resulting Eu-bpy-Ru(bpy) -x%-CuCl/X $\lfloor x=10\rfloor$ , 20, 30, 40, and 50; Eu-bpy-Ru-x%-CuCl/X) molecular composites. Among the composites, Eu-bpy-R $\mathsf{u}{-}40\%{-}\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}$ exhibited the highest efficient and selective formate production with a yield of $3040~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in $10\mathsf{h}$ , ca. 4-fold higher than that of Eu-bpy-Ru- $40\%$ (Figures 7b, c), and with a trace amount of CO generated, illustrating the significance of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ sites in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -to-formate conversion. However, 75 $\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathsf{o l}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\mathsf{c o}$ was produced using Eu-bpy-Cu as photocatalyst after $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ irradiation without producing formate (Figure 6d), which suggested the crucial role of $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p}\forall)_{3}$ photosensitizer. The importance of the Cu site and its coordination environment is further revealed. The Eubpy- $R u-40\%-C u C l_{1}$ pyridine composite, constructed by replacing X with pyridine in [bpy-CuCl/X], showed an enhancement of the CO yield $(960\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\upunderline{{\mathbf{g}}}^{-1})$ with declined HCOO− production $(1885~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\upunderline{{\mathbf{g}}}^{-1})$ . Moreover, Eubpy-Ru-Co composite, constructed by replacing the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ center with ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ center in Eubpy- $\cdot R u\mathrm{-Cu}$ , exhibited a large amount of CO and ${\sf H}_{2}$ production, but a low selectivity for formate product $(47.9\%)$ in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction (Figure 6e). The above results demonstrated the high selectivity of $[\mathsf{b p y-C u C l}_{2}]@\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}$ active composites toward ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction to solely generate formate, where the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ species and ${\mathsf{R u}}({\mathsf{b p}}\forall)_{3}$ photosensitizers both play a vital role in controlling the selectivity for formate production.
To further enhance the coenzyme NADH regeneration rate and formic acid yield, Ju, Li and Liu et al. proposed a novel MOFbased semi-artificial system.[107] In the system, a Rh complexbased electron transfer unit was chemically attached onto the linker, which was Schiff-base functionalized, of $M L-125-N H_{2}$ for NADH regeneration, and the formate dehydrogenase was further deposited onto the hydrophobic layer of the membrane by reverse filtering technique for yielding formic acid (Figure 9). The Schiff base functionalized linker in $M!!-125-N H_{2}$ acted as both a light harvesting site and a docking site for the Rh complex, electron transfer between the linker and the Rh complex anchored in the MOFs allowed easy regeneration of NADH from NAD+ with the yield of $66.4\%$ in $60~\mathsf{m i n}$ for 5 cycles under photoexcitation. Thereafter, a membrane consisting of hydrophobic polypropylene support layer and hydrophilic regenerated cellulose skin layer was employed to immobilize and protect FDH, in which the direct interactions between enzymes and a metal complex can be avoided, thereby the efficient three-phase contact of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ (gas), ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ (liquid) and the immobilized enzyme (solid) for enzymatic catalysis could be constructed. Remarkably, the enhanced production performance of formic acid from ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was achieved in the MOF-based semi-artificial system, yield of $9.5~\mathsf{m M}$ in $24\mathsf{h}$ .
Schmuki et al. established a photocatalysis-enzyme coupled system by using the photocatalytic activity of MOF MIL-125 (Ti).[106] The platform can not only realize the photo-enzymatic cascade reaction, but also monitor the reaction yield. They first synthesized hierarchical porous MIL-125 in ${\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}{\mathsf{M}}$ nanochannels. $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ nanochannel-based membranes $(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\mathsf{M})$ were selected because of their good photocatalytic activity and excellent chemical stability. Then Au NPs were modified in hpMIL-125/ $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ nanochannels, and finally active cytochrome C (CytC) was loaded into hpMIL-125/T $\mathsf{i O}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ (Figure 10). Because the diameter of pores in MIL-125 $(1.16\ \mathsf{n m})$ is smaller than the diameters of the target enzyme (CytC, ${\sim}3.7\ \mathsf{n m}_{.}^{\prime}$ ), Schmuki et al. first used an in-situ strategy to synthesize $R\%-M1L-125-N H_{2}/\uparrow10_{2}M$ in the ${\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}{\mathsf{M}}$ nanochannels $.R\%$ represents the mole percentage of $B D C-N H_{2}$ in the mixture of BDC and $B D C-N H_{2}$ ligands). Then, $B D C-N H_{2}$ ligands were decomposed by heating to prepare hpMIL-125/ $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ , creating mesoscale porosity that can load CytC. In addition, hpMIL-125/ $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ has photocatalytic activity only under UV-light irradiation, so it was modified with Au NPs to expand the photocatalytic activity to the visible light range, resulting in the improvement of its photocatalytic activity under visible light irradiation. Different from the above two systems for fixing $\mathsf{C O}_{2},$ this system reduces $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ to ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ through photocatalysis; at the same time, under the catalysis of CytC, 3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) is oxidized to positively charged oxTMB by ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ . Because there are more cations in the nanochannels, the transport flux of positive ions is amplified, and the yields of the cascade reaction can be monitored according to the changes of ionic transmembrane currents.
As mentioned above, the incorporation of MOFs with photosensitizers and/or active units might lead to the formation of heterojunctions or homojunctions in composites, which improves the light harvesting ability and/or enriches the catalytic active sites of MOF-based photocatalysts, leading to the enhanced catalytic activity. According to the location of photosensitizers and active units introduced, the design strategy of MOF-based photocatalysts can be divided into the following types: i) photosensitizers are not introduced or physically mixed with MOFs, catalytic active centers (active units) can be introduced into MOFs by binding to metal nodes or ligands or loading/encapsulating on/into the surface/pore channels, as illustrated in Figures 11a—c; ii) photosensitizers are introduced by binding to ligands, catalytic active centers are not introduced or can be introduced by binding to metal nodes or ligands or loading/encapsulating on/into the surface/pore channels into MOFs, as illustrated in Figures 11d—g; iii) photosensitizers are introduced by binding to mental nodes, catalytic active centers are not introduced, as illustrated in Figure 11h; iV) photosensitizers are introduced by encapsulating on/into the pore channels, catalytic active centers are introduced by binding to metal nodes or loading/encapsulating on/into the surface/pore channels or are not introduced, as illustrated in Figures 11i—k. The combination of photosensitizers and catalytic active centers with MOFs can produce bifunctional MOFs, thus realizing subsequently multistep photocatalysis. Some examples in the application of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction and ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution are shown in Table 1, where the types of photosensitizers and catalytic active centers of MOF-based photocatalysts and the design strategies are summarized.
## 3. Application
Numerous MOFs have exhibited considerable promise in a variety of applications, but their low stability, such as chemical stability, mechanical stability, and thermal stability,[125] has ultimately revealed them to be inappropriate for use in practical applications. On a deep commercial level, stability for MOFs serves as the cornerstone and assurance for the practical application in addition to reactivity and economy. The instability of MOFs comes from the fact that the reversibility of coordination bonds makes them easier to be at-tacked by molecules with stronger coordination ability, which leads to the collapse of the structure.[126] Until now, numerous efforts have been devoted to thermodynamically or kinetically improve the stability of MOFs through in-situ synthetic or post-synthetic method,[127-129] for example, (i) increase the strength of coordination bonds as guided by Pearson’s hard/soft acid/base (HSAB) theory, such as assembling high-valent metals (hard acid) with carboxylate ligands (hard base) and combining low-valent metals (soft acid) and azolate ligands (soft base); (ii) reduce the contact between host MOF and guest molecules, such as functionalizing hydrophobic groups to construct water-stable MOF; and (iii) increase the ligand rigidification for higher MOF stability.[126,130] Thanks to previous efforts, MOFs with good stability are now showing unrivalled prospects in artificial photosynthesis systems, such as photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, photocatalytic hydrogen evolution, and so on.
## 3.1. MOFs for photocatalytic $\mathbf{co}_{2}$ reduction
As mentioned above, solar-light-driven reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ based on MOF-based artificial photosynthesis has been considered as a promising strategy to decrease the amounts of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ released to the atmosphere and produce fuels (such as CO, ${\mathsf{C H}}_{4},$ HCOOH, etc.).[46,131-135] Since photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction involves multiple electron transfer processes with high reaction barriers, not only efficient photosensitizers but also highly active catalysts are required.[56,136-137] Moreover, the adsorption and activation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecular, charge separation and transfer, and subsequent charge utilization must be considered when designing photocatalytic systems consisting of photosensitizers and catalysts. Until now, many efforts have been focused on the development of MOF-based artificial photosynthesis systems in the past few years.[138-139]
The rational design of coordination spheres in MOF structures can provide insights into the structure−property relationships for MOF-based artificial photosynthesis. Cao et al. reported a pyrazolyl porphyrinic Ni-MOF (PCN-601) with the robust coordination spheres of pyrazolyl groups and Ni-oxo clusters for visible-lightdriven overall gas phase ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction in the presence of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ vapor.[140] For comparison, another two analogous carboxylate porphyrinic MOFs, PCN-222 and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{3}{\mathsf{T C P P}},$ bearing the same lightharvesting porphyrin unit with PCN-601 but differing in term of chelating groups and the resulted coordination spheres, were chosen to unveil the interplay between the structure and catalytic activity. In PCN-222, the high-valent Zr-Oxo metal nodes, while allowing for frame robustness, do not favor to catalytic reactions due to their high energy barriers. Though ${\mathsf{N i}}_{3}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ integrates active Ni-Oxo metal nodes into the framework, it causes serious damage to chemical stability. In PCN-601, the pyrazolyl groups possess a larger $\pi$ -conjugation system and lead to a higher degree of $\pi{-}\mathsf{d}$ orbital overlap with ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ , which is different from the above two MOFs with carboxylate groups. In the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction experiments, PCN-601 showed well reproducible activities for 5 cycles in $50\textmd h$ owing to the structural stability, and exhibited higher CO and $C H_{4}$ production rates than the other two MOFs due to its stronger coordination bonds and faster ligand-to-metal interfacial charge transfer (Figure 12a). Notably, PCN-601 possessed the highest $C H_{4}$ production rate of $10.1~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}}{\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}},$ which was almost 3 and 17 times higher than those of PCN-222 and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{3}{\mathsf{T C P P}},$ respectively. Further, the moles of effective electrons in the reaction catalyzed by PCN-601, PCN-222, and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{3}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ as well as $1\%P t/C d S$ were compared (Figure 12b), indicating that PCN-601 possessed far more photogenerated electrons for $C H_{4}$ production. The interfacial charge separation and transfer efficiency of PCN-601 and PCN-222 were evaluated via the gas-sensing currents. The results showed that the current value of PCN-601 electrode is an order of magnitude higher than that of PCN-222 (Figure 12c), illustrating the higher intrinsic charge mobility in PCN-601. Meanwhile, the much weaker photoluminescence intensity of PCN-601 verified the effective charge separation of PCN-601 (Figure 12d). Admittedly, PCN-601 has an optimized coordination sphere structure that successfully harmonizes the requirements of reactive metal components and robust framework to achieve ultrafast ligand-to-node electron transfer and efficient charge separation in photocatalysis.
Lan et al. constructed a MOF photocatalyst system (heterometallic $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ cluster-based MOFs, NNU-31-M, $M={\mathsf{C o}},$ Ni, Zn) to accomplish the full reaction of artificial photosynthesis and provided more direct and clear evidence of the structure-function relationship of the photocatalyst.[108] Heterometallic clusters and ligands are excited by visible light to generate electron-hole pairs. The electrons are then transferred to the lower valent metal for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, while the holes are transferred to the higher valent iron for ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ oxidation. For visible-light photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, the highest ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2^{-}}{\mathsf{H C O O H}}$ conversion efficiency $(26.3\upmu\mathrm{mol\cdotg}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ was shown by NNU-31-Zn, with a selectivity of ca. $100\%$ (Figures 13a, b). Under pure ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ atmosphere in aqueous solution, NNU-31-Zn showed the highest ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to HCOOH conversion efficiency, without additional photosensitizers and sacrificial agents, which means NNU-31-Zn is an efficient and selective heterogeneous photocatalyst that integrates ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ oxidation half-reactions. The results of density functional theory (DFT) and time-dependent DFT (TDDFT) calculation methods show that the Zn-containing MOFs have higher light absorption efficiency in the visible range than NU-31-Co, NU-31-Ni, and TCA $(4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -nitrilotribenzoic acid ligands), attributed to the effective charge transfer from the TCA ligand to the transition metal center, which promotes further photocatalytic reactions.
Integrating metal nanoparticles, such as Ag, Pd, Au, etc., into
MOF enables the utilization of solar energy to drive the reaction by making use of their well-known surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) effects.[141-142] For example, Yaghi et al. covalently attached a ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -to-CO conversion photocatalyst $\mathsf{R e l}(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{(B P Y D C)C l},\mathsf{B P Y D C}=$ 2,2′bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate (ReTC), to a zirconium MOF, UiO-67 $({\mathsf{R e}}_{n}{-}{\mathsf{M O F}})$ with its density in the framework (0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 11, 16, and 24 complexes per unit cell), preventing dimerization leading to deactivation, and the ${\mathsf{R e}}_{3}–{\mathsf{M O F}}$ was coated onto $\mathsf{A g}$ nanocubes $(A g C R e_{3}–M O F)$ based on the structure−activity correlation of ${\mathsf{R e}}_{n}$ -MOFs (Figure 14).[109] The ${\sf A g}$ nanocubes $98\ \mathsf{n m}$ in size) were used in the synthesis procedure of ${\mathsf{R e}}_{3}–{\mathsf{M O F}}$ with a thickness of $33\ \mathsf{n m}$ to give ${\sf A g}{\sf C R e}_{3}$ -MOF and exhibited a strong quadrupolar localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) scattering peak $(\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}\sim480\ \mathsf{n m})$ , which overlaps with the absorption range of ReTC $400\ \mathsf{n m}<\lambda<550\ \mathsf{n m})$ in the visible region. Moreover, the ${\mathsf{A g c R e}}_{3}–{\mathsf{M O F}}$ structure featured the LSPR effect of the $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}$ core and spatially confined photoactive Re centers to the intensified near-surface electric fields at the surface of Ag nanocube. Once irradiated with light, the $\mathsf{A g}$ nanocubes generated intensified near-surface electric fields at their surface plasmon resonance frequency that can be orders of magnitude higher in intensity than the incident electromagnetic field. Thus, for ${\mathsf{A g c R e}}_{3}–{\mathsf{M O F}},$ the intensified near-field created at the surface of Ag nanocubes was expected to be absorbed by ReTCs incorporated into the ${\mathsf{R e}}_{3}–{\mathsf{M O F}}$ layer for improving photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, which was 7-fold enhancement of photocatalytic activity compared with that of ${\mathsf{R e}}_{3}$ -MOF under visible light.
Even when carbon dioxide reduction occurs, the wide range of potential reduction pathways often results in the formation of multiple products. To address the issue, Zhong, Mei, and Jiang et al. synthesized a MOF-808-CuNi featuring $\mathsf{C u/N i}$ dual-metal-site pairs (DMSPs), which exhibited the dynamic ability of the enzyme to adaptively stabilize a variety of C1 intermediates during ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction, thus significantly improving the activity and selectivity for $C H_{4}$ (Figure 15).[114] Flexible ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) was firstly dangled onto the rigid $Z r$ -oxo clusters, followed by metal chelation reaction, thus MOF-808-CuNi can be yield. To demonstrate the efficient and selective superiority of MOF-808-CuNi, the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction performance of several samples was evaluated with TEOA as a sacrificial agent: i) MOF-808-EDTA, without additional metal sites, exhibited multiple products $C H_{4},$ CO, ${\sf H}_{2}$ and HCOOH) generation with low evolution rates and the HCOOH evolution rate was much higher than that of $\mathsf{C H}_{4};$ ii) In comparison, MOF-808-Ni presented only a slight decrease in the HCOOH evolution rate; iii) MOF-808-Cu displayed an increased $C H_{4}$ evolution rate and an effectively suppressed HCOOH evolution rate; iv) MOF-808-CuNi featuring dual-metalsite pairs showed a significantly enhanced $C H_{4}$ evolution rate of $158\ \mathrm{\dot{\upmu}m o l\cdot g^{-1}}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with turnover number of roughly 5.92 at $10\mathrm{~h~}$ , high $\mathsf{C H}_{4}\mathsf{S e l}_{\mathsf{e l e c t r o n}}$ of roughly $99.4\%$ and $\mathsf{S e l}_{\mathsf{p r o d u c t}}$ of roughly $97.5\%$ , while the by-products were almost negligible; v) the physical mixture, denoted mix-MOF-808-Cu-Ni, showed a much inferior $C H_{4}$ evolution rate of $47.7~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}}{\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}}$ ; Vi) MOF808-CuNi(low), where the low metal loadings could increase the distance between Cu and Ni sites, exhibited low $C H_{4}$ selectivity. Moreover, the superior $C H_{4}$ activity and selectivity can be achieved over MOF-808-CuNi, compared to that of MOF-808-MnCo, MOF-808-CuMn and MOF-808-NiCo, supporting the occurrence of a synergistic effect between Cu and Ni sites for converting CO and HCOOH to $C H_{4}$ . Photoelectrochemical measurements revealed that the incorporation of metal ions suppresses radiative electron-hole recombination and the superior separation and transport of photoinduced charges in MOF-808-CuNi. Further, both Cu and Ni sites were able to accept electrons transferring from $\big[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p}\mathsf{y})_{3}\big]\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ to $Z r$ -oxo clusters in MOF-808-CuNi, thereby activated to convert ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to $C H_{4}$ .
One of the challenges of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction is the kinetically sluggish nature of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction half-reaction involving multiple electrons. To unilaterally achieve considerable ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction activities, hole scavengers (such as triethanolamine and triethylamine) or electron donors are often used to rapidly consume photogenerated holes in situ and provide large amounts of available electro ns,[50,94] however, it limited the reaction to a liquid rather than gaseous ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ atmosphere. Many MOFs possessing capture and enrich ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ ability enable themselves to be potentially applied for the artificial photosynthesis in gaseous ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ atmosphere. For example, Zhang et al. synthesized a porous Ru−Co azolate framework, MAF-34-CoRu (the coordination and topology structures, Figure 16a), where the classic photosensitive and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ -oxidizing ruthenium bipyridyl units and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -reducing cobalt imidazolate units were incorporated into a MOF using a classic organic ligand, imidazo[4,5-f][1,10]phenanthroline.[23] The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) potentials of MAF-34-CoRu, MAF-34-Co, and $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{H i p})_{3}]\mathsf C|_{2}$ were determined by the Tauc plot of the UV−vis spectra and Mott−Schottky measurements (Figure 16b). Notably, the LUMO potential of MAF-34-CoRu was more negative than the redox potentials of $\mathsf{C O/C O}_{2}$ $(-0.53~\lor$ vs. NHE), and the HOMO potential was more positive than the redox potential of $0_{2}/\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(+0.82\vee$ vs. NHE), suggesting that the photocatalyst was thermodynamically suitable for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction with ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ oxidation. Upon visible light irradiation and without any sacrificial agents, the artificial photosynthesis experiments for MAF-34-CoRu were evaluated in a solution of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{C N/H}}_{2}0$ saturated by ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at ambient pressure. The CO and $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ production rates can be kept constant in $120{\mathsf{h}}.$ , which were 11.20 and ${\mathsf{5.57~}}{\mathsf{\upmu m o l{\cdot}g}}^{-1}{\cdot}{\mathsf{h}}^{-1},$ respectively. The mechanism of the overall photocatalytic reaction for MAF-34-
