## 3.2. MOFs for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution
Hydrogen energy with high energy density and efficient energy conversion is generally accepted as a clean energy, and its usage would contribute greatly to the transformation of the world energy pattern.[118,143] Since 1972, Fujishima and Honda reported that the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ can be used for hydrogen evolution from photocatalysis water splitting,[144] solar-driven hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) has attracted extensive attentions. MOFs, possessing the merit of high specific surface area and large pore volumes, can be used as hosts to load photosensitizers or active catalysts to expand the absorption wavelength range or obtain highly dispersed active sites, thus improving the efficiency of hydrogen evolution.[83,122,145-146]
Pt NPs are very general co-catalysts for ${\sf H}_{2}$ generation in semiconductor based photocatalysts. The same effect is illustrated also for MOF-based photocatalysts. Lin et al. encapsulated Pt NPs into the cavities of two UiO MOFs (MOFS 1 and 2) built from Ir-phosphor-derived linear dicarboxylate linkers and $Z r_{6}(\upmu_{3}{-}0)_{4}(\upmu_{3}{-}0\mathsf{H})_{4}.$ 一 (carboxylate) $\rvert_{12}$ secondary building units, via MOF-mediated photoreduction of ${\sf K}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{4}$ .[147] There is a positive correlation between the amount of ${\sf K}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{4}$ added to the reaction solution and the amount of Pt deposited in MOF (Figure 17c). The resulting ${\mathsf{P t@M O F}}$ exhibited a synergistic photocatalytic hydrogen evolution process via photoinjection of electrons from the light harvesting MOF frameworks into the Pt NPs (Figure 17a). Pt NPs with diameters of $2-3~\mathsf{n m}$ and $5-6\ \mathsf{n m}$ were formed inside the cavities of 1 and 2, respectively, and the loaded Pt NPs enabled the enhanced light harvesting capacity of original MOF (Figure 17b).
$\mathsf{P t}\ @1$ and $\mathsf{P t}(\varpi2$ samples yielded a total Ir-TON of 3400 and 7000, respectively, which were higher than that of the homogeneous controls $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]\mathsf{C l}/\mathsf{K}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{4}$ (Figure 17d), as a result of facile electron transfer from the photoreduced Ir phosphor to the entrapped Pt NPs.
In addition to monometallic nanoparticles serving as co-catalysts, Jiang et al. reported a bimetallic NPs/MOF system, $\mathsf{P d}\ @\mathsf{P t}/\$ ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-66-N H}}_{2},$ to investigate the structural influence of a ${\mathsf{P d}}\ @{\mathsf{P t}}$ co-catalyst on its surface charge state and resulting photocatalytic activity.[117] After a post-synthetic process, the core shell-structured $\mathsf{P d}_{10}@\mathsf{P t}_{x}$ $(x\ =\ 0.3$ 1, 5, 10) NPs can be stabilized by $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2},$ resulting in $\mathsf{P d}_{10}@\mathsf{P t}_{x}/\mathsf{U i O}-66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ (Figure 18). Considering the different working functions of Pd and $\mathsf{P t},$ to balance the Fermi distribution of electrons in $\mathsf{P d}@\mathsf{P t}\mathsf{N P s},$ electrons tend to move from Pd (with lower work function and weaker electron binding) to Pt (with higher work function) at the interface, resulting in the change of charge state on Pt surface. The structure of Pd@Pt NPs evolves from core-shell structure to single-atom alloy structure, which results in charge redistribution, thus affecting the surface charge state of Pt. Further, when less $\mathsf{P t}_{x}$ ( $x$ value from 10 to 1) is covered, the charge redistribution effect gradually increases, presenting the most electron-rich $\mathsf{P t}$ in $\mathsf{P d}_{10}@\mathsf{P t}_{1}$ NPs. The influence of charge redistribution in $\mathsf{P d}_{10}@\mathsf{P t}_{x}/\mathsf{U i O}{-}66{\mathsf{-N H}_{2}}$ for photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production was investigated. Remarkably, the samples $\mathsf{P d}_{10}@\mathsf{P t}_{x}/\mathsf{U i O}-66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ showed much higher photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production rates than other samples. The sample $\mathsf{P d}_{10}@$ $\mathsf{P t}_{1}/\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ possessed the most efficient ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution, 1200.5 $\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathsf{o l}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , due to the most electron-rich Pt in $\mathsf{P d}_{10}@\mathsf{P t}_{1}$ NPs and exhibited good recyclability in five consecutive runs and a continued test for $25\ h$ . Photoluminescence (PL) emission spectra and the electrochemical measurements showed that the sample $\mathsf{P d}_{10}@\mathsf{P t}_{1}/\mathsf{U i O}-66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ owned the most efficient charge separation during the photocatalytic process, which favors to the photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production.
For photosynthesis, the influence of the lifetime of the excited state should not be negligible. The longer the lifetime of the excited state, the higher the efficiency of artificial photosynthesis. However, the lifetime of the excited states is mediocre for pure MOFs, usually only tens of nanoseconds. To overcome the above issue, regulating the coordination environment by using special ligands during the design of MOFs has become an attractive alternative. As Zhang and co-workers have proposed, the UiO-MOFs (UiO67-Ir-ppy) was modified by engineering the Ir coordination center at a molecular level with coumarin 6 (Cou 6) (UiO67-Ir-Cou 6) to switch their excited state from $^{3}{\mathsf{M L C T}}$ (metal-to-ligand charge transfer) to $^3\mathrm{{\textperthousand}}$ (intraligand), resulting in enhanced sensitizing ability and longer lifetime of the excited states.[113] Further, UiO67-Ir-ppy/M and UiO67-Ir-Cou $6/\mathsf{M}$ $[{\mathsf{M}}={\mathsf{C o}}$ and $\mathsf{c u}$ ) can be afforded by introducing Cu and Co single site catalysts into the MOFs (Figure 19a). The photocatalytic hydrogen evolution yield of UiO67-Ir-Cou 6/Co can reach $26844.6~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot}{\cdot}$ within $11\mathsf{h}$ , which was 109 times higher than that of UiO67-Ir-ppy/Co (Figure 19b), suggesting the role of Co as active sites in hydrogen evolution. Moreover, after replacing Co with $\mathsf{c u}$ , the UiO67-Ir-Cou $6/\mathsf{C u}$ can efficiently and selectively convert ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ HCOOH with a record yield of $480.7~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}}{\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}}$ and a selectivity of $99.5\%$ (Figure 19c). Steady/transient spectra and other photoelectric test results showed that the UiO67-Ir-Cou 6 possessed stronger visible light harvesting ability and longer excited state lifetime compared to UiO67-Ir-ppy. And the introduction of catalytic active site can directionally and rapidly steer the electron transfer, thus, resulting in the dramatically enhanced photocatalytic activities, which provided the insight of perfect synergy between photosensitizing center and catalytic center in the MOF matrix.
Based on the appropriate work functions and energy levels, some metal oxides can act as the regulator to tune the Fermi level and the conductive/valence band level of semiconductor photocatalysts. Thus, electrons can be injected or extracted from semiconductors to produce band bending. The resulting charge imbalance results in a built-in electric field, which is critical for inhibiting the recombination of photogenerated carriers. Recently, band bending has also been revealed in MOF photocatalysts. Jiang et al. integrated the metal oxides $(\mathsf{M o O}_{3},$ and $v_{2}0_{5}$ , which feature appropriate work functions and energy levels, with MIL-125-NH2 to afford MoO3/MIL-125-NH2 and V2O5/MIL-125-NH2 composites.[42] The lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) level of MIL$125\ –\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ was $-0.6$ eV vs. NHE determined by Mott–Schottky plots, and the highest unoccupied molecular orbital (HOMO) was $+2.05$ V vs. NHE, which is more negative than the conduction band (CB) of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{3}$ and $V_{2}O_{5}$ . These demonstrated the feasibility of spontaneous transfer of electrons from MOF to metal oxide from the perspective of thermodynamics, resulting in upward band bending on MIL-125- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ surface. The surface potentials of $\mathsf{M o O}_{3},\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{5}$ and MIL- $125\ –\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ were measured by the Kelvin probe force microscope (KPFM) to determine the surface work functions. The surface potential of $M L-125-N H_{2}$ is ca. 200 and $300~\mathsf{m V},$ which is higher than those of ${\mathsf{M o o}}_{3}$ and ${\mathsf V}_{2}{\mathsf O}_{5},$ illustrating the more negative Fermi level of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ (Figure 20a). This indicates that, from the perspective of thermodynamics, the electrons would spontaneously migrate from MOF to metal oxide once closely contacted, resulting in different static potentials inside MOF and upward band bending. Meanwhile, positively charged species would be left at the surface of $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{1}2\mathsf{5}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ after electron transferring, resulting in the built-in electric field close to the MOF surface. Further, the Ti binding energy of MIL-125- $N H_{2}$ presents a negative shift (Figure 20b), which should be contributed to the electron transfer from the MOF to the metal oxide, indirectly proving the occurrence of band bending in the MOF. The surface photovoltage (SPV) spectra of $M L-125-N H_{2}$ $M O O_{3}/M l L-125\ –N H_{2}$ and $V_{2}{\sf O}_{5}/{\sf M}{\sf I L}-125-{\sf N H}_{2}$ were recorded (Figure 20c), and the composites exhibited significantly stronger SPV signals, demonstrating the increment of the hole accumulation concentration on the surface of the MOF, which presented that the band bending does occur to the MOF upon its integration with the oxides. The solidstate current–voltage $(I{-}V)$ curves of $M O O_{3}/M l L-125\ –N H_{2}$ and $V_{2}{\sf O}_{5}/{\sf M}{\sf I L}-125-{\sf N H}_{2}$ were higher and the inflection point potentials were more positive than that of sole $M L-125-N H_{2}$ (Figure 20d), which can be inferred that the higher energy is due to the presence of band bending when the MOF integrated with the oxide leading to the built-in electric field and enhanced charge separation. Therefore, the recombination of photogenerated carriers was largely suppressed even the metal oxides neither absorb photons nor accumulate electrons in the composites under visible light irradiation, thereby regulating the charge properties of the MOF. The photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production activities of $M O O_{3}/M l-$ $125\ –\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ and $V_{2}{\sf O}_{5}/{\sf M}{\sf I L}-125\mathrm{-}{\sf N H}_{2}$ were dramatically improved, which were 56 and 42 times, respectively, compared to the original MOF.
## 3.3. Others
Photocatalysis based on MOFs leading to charge separation can trigger the oxidation and reduction reactions by the holes in the LUMO and the electrons in the HOMO, respectively. However, usually only one of the above two reactions can occur in an artificial photosynthesis system, due to the limitation of thermodynamics, reaction substrate, etc. As mentioned above, the ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion are reduction reactions. Below we will present the application of photocatalysis based on MOFs in water oxidation, ${\sf N}_{2}$ fixation, organic synthesis and environment remediation.
Photocatalysis organic transformation based on MOF has attracted much research interest in recent years, such as aerobic oxidation, aza-Henry reaction, aerobic amine coupling, reduction of nitrobenzene, and so o n.[149-151] Duan et al. synthesized $A u@Z|F-8$ single- or multi-core nanocrystals by epitaxial growth or coalescence of nuclei and studied their photocatalytic activity for the oxidation of benzyl alcohol (Figure 21).[152] There was no significant difference between the XRD patterns of Au@ZIF-8 and ZIF-8. And the TEM images revealed that the major products of $A u@Z|F-8$ were core–shell structures consisting in single-core $(48\%)$ , double- and triple-core $(45\%)$ , and the others Au NPs-free ZIF-8 NCs $(7\%)$ . The size distribution of $A u@Z1F-8$ structures ranged from 135 to $200~\mathsf{n m}$ , and the diameter of Au NPs did not change significantly in all products. Upon light irradiation $(\lambda>400$ nm), the photocatalytic oxidation of the as-prepared samples for benzyl alcohol $(20~\mathsf{m L})$ was investigated in $C H_{3}C N_{3}$ solution. After $24\ h$ irradiation, the conversion rates of single- and multi-core $A u@Z|F-8$ were $25.8\%$ and $51.6\%$ , respectively, and the selectivity for benzaldehyde in both cases was $99\%$ . The plasmonic coupling between Au NPs in the multicore structure could lead to the differences in conversion rate. Under identical reaction conditions, the low conversion rate of $A u@Z1F-8$ compared to $A u-S i O_{2}$ ( $56.9\%$ conversion rate) can be ascribed to the size selectivity of ZIF-8, which limited benzyl alcohol entry into the pore close to Au, thus resulting in the reduced photocatalytic activity.
Ye et al. prepared a series of Ti-based MOFs, including $C H_{3}-M l L-125$ (Ti), OH-MIL-125 (Ti), and $N{\sf H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti), by ligands functionalization to extend the light absorption of MIL-125(Ti) to visible region for the photocatalytic $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ fixation (Figure 22).[153] Among the functionalized Ti-based MOFs, $N{\sf H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) exhibited the highest photocatalytic conversion rate for ${\sf N}_{2}$ to $N H_{3}$ $\cdot12.3\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ upon visible light irradiation, which can be attributed to the presence of amine functionality that enhanced the light harvesting and facilitated the electrons transfer to the Ti-oxo clusters for reduction of ${\sf N}_{2}$ molecules. The Ti species in $N H_{2}-M L-125$ (Ti) can be reduced from $\upuparrows^{4+}$ to $\mathfrak{T i}^{3+}$ by the photoexcited electrons transferring from $N H_{2}-B D C$ ligands through LMCT mechanism during the reaction. Subsequently, the photogenerated electrons were directly injected into the antibonding $\pi$ -orbitals of the chemisorbed ${\sf N}_{2}$ on the $\upuparrows^{3+}$ sites via electron donation/electron exchange reactions, thus the molecular ${\sf N}_{2}$ triple bond $(N\equiv N)$ can be weakened, facilitating the transformation of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ to $N{\sf H}_{3}$ . Further, no ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ can be detected for $N{\sf H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) during the reaction process as the aqueous solution was replaced with DMF solvent, revealing the importance of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ as an electron/proton donor.
MOF-based photocatalysis has become one of the most promising strategies for environmental remediation, including wastewater treatment (e.g., organic pollutants and heavy metal ions), air purification (e.g., gaseous $\mathsf{N O}_{x})$ , etc.[37,154] For example, Wu et al. presented a series of ${\mathsf{H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P M o}}_{12}{\mathsf{O}}_{40}@$ MIL-100(Fe) composites by encapsulating ${\mathsf{H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P M o}}_{12}{\mathsf{O}}_{40}$ into MIL-100(Fe) for photocatalytic reduction of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V}|)$ and selective oxidation of benzylic alcohols.[155] The as-prepared $5\%$ $\%\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}@$ MIL-100(Fe) sample showed the highest photocatalytic activity for $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V}|)$ reduction, almost $100\%\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ can be reduced after 8 min of visible light irradiation in $40~\mathsf{m L}$ of $20\mathsf{m g}{\cdot}\mathsf{L}^{-1}\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V}|)$ solution. Moreover, under visible light irradiation, $30\%$ $\gamma_{\circ}\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}@\mathsf{M}\mathsf{I}\mathsf{L}\mathsf{-}100(\mathsf{F e}$ ) sample exhibited photocatalytic activity for selective oxidation of a range of benzylic alcohols to their corresponding aldehydes (conversion rate $18\%-29\%$ and selectivity $>90\%$ . The $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}@\mathsf{M L}$ - 100(Fe) composites exhibiting higher photocatalytic activities can be attributed to the enhanced light absorption and the efficient separation of photogenerated charges. The authors believed that ${\mathsf H}_{3}{\mathsf P}{\mathsf M}{\mathsf0}_{12}{\mathsf0}_{40}$ and MIL-100(Fe) acted as catalytic active sites during alcohol substrate oxidation and $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V}|)$ reduction, respectively.
## 4. Conclusions and Perspectives
MOF-based artificial photosynthesis (AP) systems have attracted a lot of attention, and recent years have witnessed their significant achievements in the application of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution, organic synthesis, environment remediation, etc. Inspired by natural photosynthesis systems, semi-artificial photosynthesis (SAP) systems constructed by coupling biological enzymes and MOFs have spurred new research interest very recently, and it can be predicted that they will flourish in the near future. The catalytic efficiency, selectivity and stability of artificial photosynthesis (AP) and semi-artificial photosynthesis (SAP) systems are closely related to the light absorption ability, charges separation and transfer efficiency, the type, number and spatial arrangement of active sites of MOF-based photocatalysts. It is exciting that MOFs featuring large specific surface area, high porosity and the capability of precisely designing and tailoring structures endow the opportunity for clever and rational design of photosensitizer and catalytic active center on MOF, which has led to a variety of combination strategies of photosensitizer and catalytic center in spatial location. Table 1 summarizes detailed information on the photosensitizers, catalytic active centers and synthetic strategies of representative MOF-based composite photocatalysts in the application of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction and ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution, providing guidance for the development of highly efficient MOFbased photocatalysts.
Though the development of MOF-based artificial photosynthesis system has been greatly advanced over the past few years, the efficiency and selectivity of the MOF-based photocatalysts are still very low, indicating that they are not yet effective enough for industrial applications and further efforts are needed to achieve great breakthroughs. First, most current artificial photosynthesis systems focus only on half reaction (oxidation only or reduction only) and rely on uneconomical and environmentally unfriendly sacrificial agents (e.g., TEOA and TEA), unlike natural photosynthesis where water oxidation reaction occurs in conjunction with carbon dioxide reduction. Therefore, MOF-based photocatalysts with well-defined structure, where the oxidation and reduction active sites are coupled, but in different spatial locations, should be explored to trigger the overall photosynthesis reaction with high efficiency and selectivity in one system. Second, most of the solar-driven ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction products based on MOF artificial photosynthesis are $\mathsf C_{1}$ products (e.g., $C H_{4},$ CO, HCOOH), while the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ coupling products with high added value (e.g., ${\mathsf C}_{2}{\mathsf H}_{6})$ via a kinetically sluggish multielectron process are rarely reported. Thus, the design and synthesis of MOF-based photocatalysts for high value-added ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction products, such as $\mathsf C_{2+},$ remains to be explored. Third, the employment of semi-artificial photosynthesis systems coupled with biological enzymes or bacteria and MOFs to catalyze the synthesis of higher value carbohydrates will have broad development prospects in the next decades due to the higher specificity, selectivity and self-repair properties of semiartificial system than artificial photosynthesis system.
Great progress has been made in MOF-based artificial and semi-artificial photosynthesis systems, but they are still far from large-scale commercial applications. From the increasing research interest and abundant efforts, it is foreseeable that artificial and semi-artificial photosynthesis systems will achieve the required efficiency, selectivity and stability.
